--TEST--
A script that ended with `Core\Script::finish()` is an ordinary end, so its `Core\Task::afterResponse` work still runs — `rule:concurrency/after-response-outlives-the-connection` and `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The classification, asserted where it is observable. § 6 gives deferred work
// to a request that ended ordinarily and to no other: a throw, an `exit` and a
// `FATAL` each run none of it, because the host is about to report that status
// and script running over it would lose one of the two. A finish arrives on the
// same path a throw does and is *not* that — the handler is done answering, not
// failing — so the queue drains and the deferred callable runs.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "then the receipt\n";
});
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    echo "the queue, before the deferred work\n";
});

// A CLI run has no response, so the script's own frame returning is when
// "after the response" is — and `finish` is what brings that moment forward.
echo "answered\n";
Core\Script::finish();
echo "this line never runs\n";
--EXPECT--
answered
the queue, before the deferred work
then the receipt
